  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT WED SEP 9 2026\n\nThis evening, an upper shortwave digging around the southwest\nside of an Eastern Canada main low will eat away at the upper\nridge across the Southern US tonight. This will create enough of\na northwest flow to push a surface front close to Alabama by\nearly Friday as the parent associated surface low moves eastward\nacross and out of Quebec. As it does so, the front will move\nacross portions of the Mississippi River Valley, Ohio Valley and \nNew England. However, don't look for pumpkin spiced latte weather \njust yet. Low rain chances across mainly the southern counties of \nAlabama on Thursday will give way to medium to high rain chances \nfor Friday into the weekend for all of Central Alabama. The main \nenergy from the surface low will push eastward into the Atlantic \non Friday with the boundary pushing back well to the north, giving\nway to a moist southerly fetch into the state. In the upper levels,\npositively tilted troughing shifts closer to Alabama with ample \ndisturbance activity expected for Friday and Saturday across \nCentral Alabama. While the probability for widespread flooding is \nlow, QPF amounts of 1 to 2 inches may occur over this time frame \nwith a few locally isolated higher amounts.\n\nBy Sunday, the trough weakens as the upper ridge builds across\nthe eastern half of the Southern US. While there is still a\nmedium chance for activity to occur, it should be more scattered\nin nature with lower amounts overall. By Monday, and on into the\nmiddle part of next week, the upper ridge is expected to\nstrengthen further across East Central Conus. This will yield an\neasterly flow into Central Alabama and more diurnally enhanced\nisolated to scattered convective activity once again with low QPF\namounts.\n\n08",
